#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209870} :activity/id #uuid "e03cea40-d542-50a2-beef-0257307c0a4f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050484 :topic/id #uuid "4d4dd9cc-d32a-5514-bbc8-b9d4357f3376" :topic/math-academy-id 6007} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209878 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll extend our knowledge of gradients of scalar functions to include quadratic functions, quadratic forms, and the chain rule.\n\nConsider the (scalar) **quadratic function**\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{A} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}$ is a matrix, and $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ is a column vector. We wish to calculate the gradient of this function:\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf x} f = \\nabla_{\\mathbf x} \\left(\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe start by showing that $f$ can be written in the expanded form\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_i x_j\n$$\n\nTo understand why this is the same as $\\mathbf x^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf x$, let's first write\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we compute $\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}$ by working step by step.\n\nFirst, we compute the product $\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x}$, which gives us an $n\\times 1$ column vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAx &= \\begin{bmatrix}a_{11} & a_{12} & ⋯ & a_{1n} \\\\ a_{21} & a_{22} & ⋯ & a_{2n} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ a_{n1} & a_{n2} & ⋯ & a_{nn}\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ x_{n}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}a_{11}x_{1} + a_{12}x_{2} + ⋯ + a_{1n}x_{n} \\\\ a_{21}x_{1} + a_{22}x_{2} + ⋯ + a_{2n}x_{n} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ a_{n1}x_{1} + a_{n2}x_{2} + ⋯ + a_{nn}x_{n}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}∑_(j = 1)^(n)a_{1j}x_{j} \\\\ ∑_(j = 1)^(n)a_{2j}x_{j} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ ∑_(j = 1)^(n)a_{nj}x_{j}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply $\\mathbf{x}^T$ by this result:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & x_{2} & ⋯ & x_{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nEach term of this product looks like\n\n$$\nx_i \\cdot \\displaystyle\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_j\n$$\n\n, so the full expression becomes\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} = \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \\left(\\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_j \\right) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_i x_j\n$$\n\nSo, we've shown that\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{x} = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij} x_i x_j.}\n$$\n\nLet's now discuss how this helps us to compute the gradient of $f$." :tutorial/id #uuid "46d7287c-3842-57d9-8f8b-8f5f95c5362f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12386 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b43433a0-9dff-53f0-b8cf-64336b4b2fc7" :step/math-academy-id 610963 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209871}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209871 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209954 :tutorial/content "We have the quadratic function\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n \\times n}$ is a matrix, and $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^n$ is a column vector. As we've seen, $f$ can be written in the expanded form\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\sum_{j=1}^n A_{ij} x_i x_j\n$$\n\nWe'll use this form to compute the gradient of $f$.\n\nTo compute the gradient $\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f$, we'll differentiate this expanded form with respect to $x_k$. First, we express our expanded function as\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf x) = \\sum_{i \\neq k} \\sum_{j \\neq k} A_{ij} x_i x_j + \\sum_{i \\neq k} A_{ik} x_i x_k + \\sum_{j \\neq k} A_{kj} x_k x_j + A_{kk} x_k^2\n$$\n\nThe idea here is to break up the double summation by isolating all the terms that involve the variable $x_k$.\n\nNotice that\n\n- the first summation is *independent* of $x_k$,\n- the second and third summations are *linear* in $x_k$, and\n- the final term is *quadratic* in $x_k$.\n\nWe can now compute our derivative term by term in a structured way. Differentiating with respect to $x_k$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{k}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x_{k}}[∑_(i \\ne k)∑_(j \\ne k)A_{ij}x_{i}x_{j} + ∑_(i \\ne k)A_{ik}x_{i}x_{k} + ∑_(j \\ne k)A_{kj}x_{k}x_{j} + A_{kk}x_{k}^{2}] \\\\\n&= ∑_(i \\ne k)A_{ik}x_{i} + ∑_(j \\ne k)A_{kj}x_{j} + 2A_{kk}x_{k} \\\\\n&= (∑_(i \\ne k)A_{ik}x_{i}) + A_{kk}x_{k} + (∑_(j \\ne k)A_{kj}x_{j}) + A_{kk}x_{k} \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)A_{ik}x_{i} + ∑_(j = 1)^(n)A_{kj}x_{j} \\\\\n&= (A^{T}x)_{k} + (Ax)_{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is true for all\n\n$$\n1\\leq k \\leq n\n$$\n\n, we get the result\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}) = A^T \\mathbf{x} + A \\mathbf{x} = (A^T + A)\\mathbf x\n$$\n\nFrom here, we have the following special cases:\n\n- If $A$ is symmetric, then $A^T = A$, $f(\\mathbf x)$ is a quadratic form, and our result simplifies to\n$\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{x}^T A \\mathbf{x}) = 2 A \\mathbf{x}$.\n- Suppose that $A = I_n$ is the $n\\times n$ identity matrix. Then, our result for symmetric matrices simplifies to\n$\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} (\\mathbf{x}^T \\mathbf{x}) = 2 \\mathbf{x}$\nwhich is a result we saw in a previous lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "332e4bab-6a1d-5868-a616-4a2c034c2619" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13115 :tutorial/title "The Gradient of a Quadratic Function"} :step/id #uuid "b7685ad5-7aa7-532e-936b-bd0ad89e40bd" :step/math-academy-id 610964 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209872}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209872 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208846 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208271 :question/id #uuid "a06524d0-6980-52eb-b5fb-2ca7d0c1dbac" :question/math-academy-id "e-17350"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fe0d364-ef22-5138-9ee5-e473bd909447" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049450}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208925}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Gradient of a Quadratic Form"} :step/id #uuid "1f3213b1-5ea3-5cd6-9b86-84bd1ef095b3" :step/math-academy-id 610965 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209873}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209873 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208847 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208289 :question/id #uuid "b4e84c40-15d1-585f-84c7-c615979cd8f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-18713"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2198cf08-3a99-5e5d-80af-bbe722b51647" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208957}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Gradient of a Quadratic Function"} :step/id #uuid "bc662f29-b540-527e-bb28-752fc9820d44" :step/math-academy-id 610968 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209874}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209874 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209877 :tutorial/content "Now, let $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^q$ and $f: \\mathbb{R}^q \\to \\mathbb{R}$. Suppose we want to compute the gradient\n$\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x}))$.\nTo do this, we need to apply the chain rule. The chain rule for total derivatives tells us that\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{1 \\times n} = \\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}}_{1 \\times q} \\cdot \\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{q \\times n}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the gradient of a scalar function is the transpose of the total derivative:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f}_{n \\times 1} = \\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n$$\n\nTo derive the chain rule in terms of gradients, we apply the identity\n\n$$\n(AB)^T = B^T A^T\n$$\n\n, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇_{x}f &= (\\frac{df}{dx})^{T} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{df}{dy} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx})^{T} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{dy}{dx})^{T}_(⏟)_(n \\cdot q) \\cdot (\\frac{df}{dy})^{T}_(⏟)_(q \\cdot 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient chain rule is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f = \\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T \\cdot \\nabla_{\\mathbf{y}} f\n$$\n\nNote that the order matters; when expressed in the gradient form, the derivatives are applied *from right to left!*" :tutorial/id #uuid "eb90df7d-906c-5d50-a61a-40222511f26e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12301 :tutorial/title "Computing Gradients With the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "42b51395-66f8-5f41-b3b6-f3e548175320" :step/math-academy-id 610971 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209875}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209875 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208848 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208272 :question/id #uuid "fce0099e-b010-50a4-bf0d-51aca4380129" :question/math-academy-id "e-17865"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c77271f-3d21-5193-a1ad-5c0264e337f0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048367}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186208930}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule to Gradients"} :step/id #uuid "2a7727ac-4542-5d23-af4c-7a22c6bc3047" :step/math-academy-id 610972}] :activity/title "Further Vector Gradients" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186208872}]}
